+# Chicago Lead Service Line Replacement Brief
+
+**Research cutoff: 2026-08-15.**
+
+## Decision summary
+
+Chicago should treat lead service line replacement as a citywide public-health capital program, not a collection of homeowner applications. The city has an unusually large starting inventory: EPA has reported nearly 400,000 lead service lines. Meanwhile, the federal [Lead and Copper Rule Improvements](https://www.epa.gov/ground-water-and-drinking-water/lead-and-copper-rule-improvements) (LCRI) makes complete replacement the governing direction, with most systems expected to finish within ten years and high-burden systems potentially eligible for deferred schedules.
+
+Chicago should establish one accountable office; deliver no-cost, block-by-block replacements in priority areas; and publish progress, cost, access, and quality data. Newark, New Jersey, shows how centralized finance and access authority accelerate a voluntary program, but its audits warn that speed needs independent verification. Chicago should pair Newark’s model with audits, household protection, and an equity rule.
+
+## Current policy baseline
+
+Current binding requirements must be separated from proposals. EPA’s final Lead and Copper Rule Improvements were published in 2024. The [agency’s current questions and answers](https://www.epa.gov/ground-water-and-drinking-water/lcri-questions-and-answers) say systems generally begin LCRI compliance on **November 1, 2027**. The rule requires full replacement of lead and galvanized-requiring-replacement lines under utility control within a **10-year** period for most systems, while allowing a formula-based deferred deadline for systems with exceptionally high proportions of affected lines. Illinois, not the City Council, will make relevant primacy and feasibility determinations; Chicago should therefore model both the default and any legally available deferred schedule rather than present either as settled.
+
+Some duties already apply. EPA’s [January 2026 compliance advisory](https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/compliance-advisory-failure-comply-certain-new-safe-drinking-water-act-lead-and-copper) explains that the retained 2021 rule provisions required initial inventories, notices to people served by known or possible lead lines, and rapid public notice of an action-level exceedance beginning October 16, 2024. Illinois’s [Lead Service Line Replacement and Notification Act information](https://epa.illinois.gov/topics/drinking-water/public-water-users/lead-service-line-information.html) likewise describes the state inventory and replacement-plan framework effective since 2022.
+
+The LCRI direction is full replacement. EPA’s [service-line inventory and replacement fact sheet](https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPURL.cgi?Dockey=P101C1F0.txt) says a line counts as fully replaced only when its entire customer and system sides are non-lead, restricts partial replacement, and requires public inventories and replacement plans. These are minimum legal duties, not proof that a particular local funding mechanism, neighborhood sequence, or production target has federal approval. EPA’s live 2026 [implementation-tools page](https://www.epa.gov/dwreginfo/lead-and-copper-rule-implementation-tools) continues to publish implementation materials.
+
+## Chicago conditions
+
+Scale is Chicago’s defining constraint. An [EPA account of its Chicago lead roundtable](https://www.epa.gov/newsreleases/epa-chicago-leaders-discuss-lead-drinking-water) reports nearly 400,000 lead services. Even a twenty-year path implies roughly 20,000 completions annually; a ten-year path would be about 40,000. Those are planning arithmetic, not a finding that either deadline has been assigned. The council should request a public reconciliation of known lead, galvanized, non-lead, and unknown records before adopting a production promise.
+
+Chicago has useful foundations. Municipal law defines a full line as both public and private portions and authorizes city funding of full replacements in [Chicago Municipal Code section 11-12-900](https://codelibrary.amlegal.com/codes/chicago/latest/chicago_il/0-0-0-2657067). EPA also lists an invited **$336 million** WIFIA loan for Chicago’s [water-main and associated lead-line program](https://www.epa.gov/wifia/chicago-water-main-and-lead-service-line-replacement-program). Neither fact establishes the program’s total cost, financing close, or number of lines completed. The city should publish those figures rather than infer them from announcements.
+
+Delivery involves trade-offs. Bundling lines with water-main work can reduce repeated street disruption, while exclusive bundling can strand high-risk blocks whose mains are not scheduled. Voluntary enrollment respects ordinary owner coordination, but peer-reviewed evidence on a replacement program found that low-income subsidies increased participation only when easy to use and well publicized, with meaningful nonparticipation remaining ([Klemick et al., 2024](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10640-023-00836-9)). Construction itself can temporarily disturb lead-bearing scale, so filters, flushing instructions, and follow-up sampling must accompany production rather than be treated as outreach extras.
+
+## Lessons from the comparator
+
+Newark replaced voluntary, partly owner-paid enrollment with a centralized, no-cost model. The Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago’s [Newark case study](https://www.chicagofed.org/research/content-areas/lead/newark-nj) reports about **24,000 replacements in three years**, a **$195 million** program cost (about **$8,000 per line**), and $120 million in debt financing repaid from a revised Port Authority lease revenue stream. Newark’s ordinance enabled access when absentee ownership and renter status blocked consent. The transferable principles are stable funding, full-line work, block sequencing, and a single delivery authority—not the assumption that Chicago can copy Newark’s unit cost or legal mechanism.
+
+Newark also demonstrates why completion data need verification. The New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection’s [Newark audit page](https://dep.nj.gov/newarklead/) reports that a 2024 targeted audit excavated 403 lines and found 33 properties with remaining lead components; a separate randomized audit excavated 388 and found two with remaining lead plus one galvanized line. The identified components were replaced. Chicago should preserve contractor photos and material records but validate them through independent sampling and excavation.
+
+Finally, replacement does not eliminate the need for monitoring. A peer-reviewed review of Newark found that limited and uneven compliance sampling could miss risk and documented 23,800 replacements in three years ([Stratton et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.1002/wat2.1620)). Chicago’s policy should therefore pair construction counts with water-quality surveillance and clearly label what remains uncertain inside premise plumbing.
+
+## Recommended Chicago action plan
+
+1. **Create one accountable delivery office in the first 90 days.** Owner: the Department of Water Management (DWM), with the public-health and procurement departments embedded. Publish a baseline inventory reconciliation, ten- and deferred-year production scenarios, staffing needs, and funding gap within six months.
+
+2. **Adopt a no-cost, full-line priority program within nine months.** Owner: DWM and the City Council. Sequence contiguous blocks using line status, children under six, elevated blood-lead burden, income, renter share, and planned street work. Maintain a separate risk-triggered route so bundling does not exclude vulnerable blocks.
+
+3. **Secure a blended capital plan before the 2027 compliance date.** Owner: the chief financial officer. Combine available federal and state revolving support, WIFIA financing, water revenues, and locally authorized debt; publish grant-versus-loan assumptions, rate impacts by household income, and a contingency range. Do not condition priority-area work on an owner contribution.
+
+4. **Procure geographic work packages with verification beginning in year one.** Owner: the procurement department and inspector general. Pay against full-line milestones, restoration, and validated records; reserve final payment until quality checks close. Use multiple packages and apprenticeship commitments to reduce single-contractor and labor-capacity risk.
+
+5. **Protect households before, during, and after every replacement.** Owner: DWM and the Chicago Department of Public Health. Provide multilingual notice, certified filters and cartridges, flushing directions, a named contact, and scheduled follow-up sampling. Report results without implying that a service-line replacement removes every possible premise-plumbing source.
+
+## Equity and accountability measures
+
+- **Inventory:** classify at least **95%** of unknown service lines within **24 months**, publishing unknown rates by census tract and tenure each quarter.
+- **Priority delivery:** direct at least **60%** of annual replacements during the first **3 years** to the highest-risk quartile under the adopted public index; report both assigned and completed work.
+- **Affordability and access:** complete **100%** of priority-program replacements without a resident charge, track at least **4** contact attempts through **2** methods where access stalls, and publish renter-versus-owner completion gaps.
+- **Quality assurance:** independently audit at least **5%** of completed lines plus **100%** of exception flags; correct confirmed residual lead within **30 days** and report contractor-specific error rates.
+- **Production and cost:** report completions, full-versus-partial status, median days, restoration backlog, and cost per validated line every quarter; explain any annual volume or unit-cost variance above **10%**.
+- **Health protection:** document filter delivery and flushing education for **100%** of affected households and publish follow-up sampling completion and results by neighborhood, while protecting household privacy.
+
+## Draft committee statement
+
+Chair and members of the committee, I ask you to establish a single, publicly accountable program to replace Chicago’s lead service lines in full, without charging households in the neighborhoods selected for priority work.
+
+Chicago begins with a burden unlike that of most water systems: approximately 400,000 lead service lines. Federal rules now point toward complete replacement, with a November 1, 2027 compliance date for the new framework and a ten-year default replacement period for most systems. Chicago may qualify for a longer formula-based schedule, but uncertainty about that determination is a reason to prepare transparent scenarios now, not a reason to wait.
+
+Newark offers both a model and a warning. Centralized financing, no-cost full replacements, block-by-block contracting, and authority to resolve access barriers helped Newark complete roughly 24,000 lines in three years. Later audits also found some properties where lead components remained. Our lesson should be to pair urgency with independent quality control.
+
+Please require the Department of Water Management to return within six months with a reconciled inventory, production scenarios, workforce plan, and financing gap. Direct the program to prioritize blocks using transparent measures of line risk, young children, blood-lead burden, income, and renter status. Protect residents with filters, flushing guidance, multilingual notice, and follow-up sampling. Finally, require a public dashboard that reports full replacements, costs, access barriers, neighborhood equity, and independent audit results every quarter.
+
+This is not simply a construction count. It is a public-health commitment and a test of whether city infrastructure dollars reach residents who face the greatest risk and the least ability to navigate a voluntary application. Adopt a no-cost full-line policy, fund a delivery office capable of meeting it, and make every claimed completion verifiable.
+
